WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.450
- Okay.

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:02.490
Wow.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.323
I'm, like, kind of nervous.

00:00:03.323 --> 00:00:04.710
This is crazy.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:05.552
Alright.

00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:08.135
(lively music)

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.680
Can you see what's inside this circle?

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.350
If not, don't worry.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:16.183
Neither can I.

00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:18.610
Chances are, you're probably
colorblind just like me.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.890
The only time my colorblindness
is really an issue

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:21.723
is when I'm editing

00:00:21.723 --> 00:00:23.220
and I have to do color correction--

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.490
- So, are you color correcting right now?

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:26.323
- Yeah.

00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:28.540
- Does it look red at all to you?

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.260
The answer is that it's very red.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.010
- It doesn't, that doesn't look red.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:32.843
I'm sorry.
(laughing)

00:00:32.843 --> 00:00:33.676
Like, it doesn't.

00:00:33.676 --> 00:00:35.130
There's the occasional
moment where I may have

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.980
gotten something confused.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:37.990
- I wear this color.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.090
I don't wear this color.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.435
- They're the same color!

00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:41.268
- They are not the same color.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.460
- They...

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.240
I've been colorblind my entire life.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.580
I didn't really think that I

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.770
was missing out on anything.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.220
I can still see color.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.053
I just don't see color

00:00:50.053 --> 00:00:51.440
the same as everybody else.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.590
About a year and a half ago,

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.000
I heard about this company called EnChroma

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.850
that makes these glasses that are supposed

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.830
to help colorblind people see color.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.950
I saw these videos that kept coming out

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.780
of people trying the glasses

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.820
and having these big
emotional experiences,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.610
like they were seeing something amazing

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.780
for the very first time.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.150
And with all of this excitement,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.450
I realized I had to at
least try these glasses.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.300
So, I grabbed Becky and we
headed up to Berkley, California,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.653
to tour the company and, of
course, try out the glasses.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.300
- I have a company and we're
making laser-safety eyewear.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.790
And one of the features of these eyewear

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.220
was that it greatly enhanced colors.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.440
Just sort of a side effect.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.540
The surgeons loved it so much

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.360
they were stealing them. (laugh)

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.487
So I started wearing them and was like,

00:01:34.487 --> 00:01:35.610
"Oh, these work great!"

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.420
I'm an avid Ultimate Frisbee player.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.860
And I was at a tournament
in Santa Cruz, California.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.720
At the tournament, my
friend borrowed the glasses.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.647
And he said, quote, "Dude!

00:01:44.647 --> 00:01:45.480
"These are awesome!

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.700
"I can see the cones!"

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.410
Turns out he was colorblind
and he couldn't differentiate

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.280
the fluorescent orange field
marker cones from the grass.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.050
- The orange cones

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.253
and the grass?
- Yes, and the green

00:01:56.253 --> 00:01:57.760
were the same to him

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.100
until he put the glasses on.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.953
Then he could see these,

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.377
the cones clearly.

00:02:01.377 --> 00:02:04.440
(lively music)

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.230
Well, the lenses that I had made

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.960
absorb our laser function.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.810
Eventually we figured out it was because

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.730
they were creating separation
between the photo pigments.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.540
I read everything I could on the subject.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.930
Most prevalent form is
Red-Green colorblindness,

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.140
which is what 90-some percent of everybody

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.280
who has, who's colorblind,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.470
is called Red-Green.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.660
And that's hereditary.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.860
Males have like a 50-50 chance.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.440
- I fell into that 50% because
my grandfather was colorblind

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.720
and passed that trait down to my mother,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.020
who then gave it to me.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.870
In fact, my grandfather
actually found a way to cheat

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.890
the colorblind test to get into the Navy.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.880
What exactly is it that's
happening inside somebody's eye

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.030
that makes them colorblind?

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:43.863
- To really understand,

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:44.790
you have to understand how

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.290
normal color vision works
- Okay.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.950
- Because you have three photo pigments.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.440
You can call them blue, green, and red.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.380
The red and the green one

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.950
actually are right next to each other.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.660
The normal color vision,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.670
the red and green
sensitive photo pigments,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.700
have some overlap.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.950
In the abnormal form, for color blindness,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:02:59.783
they overlap too much.

00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:01.530
The more they overlap,
the more severe it gets.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.830
- And what do the glasses
do to someone like me,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.890
that's, you know, red-green deficient?

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.880
Does that kind of separate them more

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.020
so you can see the difference

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.230
between the red and the green?

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.240
- If you cut-out
wavelengths that correspond

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.640
to where there's too much overlap,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.440
you recreate a separation.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.260
You reestablish normal capture of photons.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.610
And now, all those mechanisms

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.750
that are in here start to function.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:24.840
It's like, oh!
- Yeah.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.020
- Yhat's clearly different.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.280
Now the question is, can you,
are you seeing a difference

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.054
or are you actually seeing red and green?

00:03:30.054 --> 00:03:32.000
(light music)

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.480
- We were driving past this thing

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.500
and I had to check it out.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.333
So, here we go.

00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.100
This is the first time me
putting on the glasses.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:37.933
These look like some cool,

00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:39.250
like, Ray-Ban wayfarers.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.083
I'm into that.

00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:40.916
I'm like, kind of nervous.

00:03:40.916 --> 00:03:42.193
This is crazy.

00:03:42.193 --> 00:03:44.322
(inhales deeply)
Alright.

00:03:44.322 --> 00:03:45.155
Whoa!

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.560
Whoa!

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.110
This dress, it looks so blue!

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.650
- That?
- That's green!

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:55.900
And pink!

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.370
I couldn't see that green before!

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.523
I thought it was gray.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.770
Wow, okay.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:03.603
Let's keep moving!

00:04:03.603 --> 00:04:04.436
Let's go!

00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:05.269
Let's see what's next!

00:04:05.269 --> 00:04:06.970
(upbeat music)

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:10.300
The trees and then the grass on the hill

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.520
and then the red,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.830
and then the brown,

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:13.740
and then the green,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.340
and then the blue.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.070
There's so many colors!

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.800
Oh my gosh!

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.160
This has multiple shades of green in it.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.510
It's like being on an acid trip,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:23.818
from what I've heard.

00:04:23.818 --> 00:04:28.818
Wow. (exhales deeply)

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.750
I can't believe that this is
what everybody else gets to see

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:34.710
on a regular basis.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.800
To see this,

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.193
to see why people cry
at sunsets sometimes.

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:43.060
I don't know if I've ever seen

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:43.902
anything as beautiful.

00:04:43.902 --> 00:04:46.985
(light upbeat music)

